WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Steve from&nbsp;
the San Francisco Bay area. Steve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.400
welcome. What's on your mind today?
Speaker 5: Hey, David, nice to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:15.300
to you again. I was I was trying to think about&nbsp;
how we can get more taxes out of corporations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:21.600
you know, because historically we're at a pretty&nbsp;
low rate right now. And we don't also want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.920
disincentivize them and, you know, send them&nbsp;
away. I don't know if that's a real argument.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:32.580
But anyways, I was thinking about an idea where&nbsp;
perhaps there was some sort of sliding scale as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:37.369
far as the tax rate relative to corporations&nbsp;
profit. What do you think about that idea?&nbsp;

00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:41.820
Speaker 1: You're basically just talking about&nbsp;
a progressive bracketed system for corporations.&nbsp;

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:48.000
Speaker 5: Yeah, basically like certain industries&nbsp;
of course have very high profit margins like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.940
um, like the beauty industry and things like&nbsp;
that. Others not so much, but I'm saying like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.680
hey, if you're making huge multiples&nbsp;
of your, your revenue, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.780
maybe you have a higher tax rate than a&nbsp;
company that's making a smaller tax area.&nbsp;

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:08.040
Speaker 1: I don't know that I would do it by&nbsp;
industry, but I actually think there is lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.360
hanging fruit. And this is something that a lot&nbsp;
of Democratic lawmakers have tried to go for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:18.000
which is we need to have a minimum tax for&nbsp;
corporations. And this has been part of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.980
number of different proposals. And to be&nbsp;
honest, I don't remember if it's part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:31.740
what Biden wants to do. The idea is you can't&nbsp;
deduct your way all the way down to zero taxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.780
the way some corporations have done. And part&nbsp;
of what you do is you have to eliminate certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.960
mechanisms as it sort of beyond the scope of this&nbsp;
particular recall. But, you know, before we look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.780
into progressive tax rates for corporations,&nbsp;
there are very profitable corporations that pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.340
literally zero or have a negative tax rate that&nbsp;
needs to be eliminated with something equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.560
to the alternative minimum tax for businesses.
Speaker 5: Oh, yeah, for sure. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.760
places like Amazon are famous for that kind&nbsp;
of thing. Absolutely. Yeah. That's a very good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.460
solution. And I can get behind that.
Speaker 1: All right, Steve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.980
thank you. Everything good in the Bay Area?
Speaker 5: Yeah. It's nice. In about 65 today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.260
enjoying the California weather?
Speaker 1: Oh, that's a great temperature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.369
It's hard to beat 65 in my book.
Speaker 3: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.050
Speaker 1: All right, Steve, thank you.
Speaker 3: You bet.&nbsp;

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.740
Speaker 1: All right. The San Francisco&nbsp;
Bay Area, always great to check in.

